+# City bike shortcut and speed
+
+In safe Taichung outdoor exploration a fresh B press uses the existing
+registered Bike item task to mount or dismount. The browser's existing D and
+secondary touch mapping therefore supplies the shortcut. A remains interaction;
+Start/Select keep priority. The normal Bike/Get off menu action remains.
+
+One owner handles field input and speed. A B-up field frame arms the shortcut;
+a held press toggles once. Script/control locks, full-screen callback gaps,
+ambiguous A/Start/Select input and unsafe movement clear pending input. A short
+midstep tap can wait at most 32 frames for an ordinary tile center. Native
+surfing, indoor, rail, forced movement, cycling-road and terrain restrictions
+remain. Native registered-item task cleanup owns music and control recovery.
+
+`TaichungCanToggleBike()` is the shared contextual eligibility predicate used
+by the handler and navigation flag. It does not consume input; a fresh press
+still requires release/arming. The native field handler owns button semantics,
+so browser code must not independently simulate a mount after Back.
+
+Normal allowed city cycling selects a dedicated three-frame movement action:
+5 + 5 + 6 pixels, exactly one 16-pixel tile, using the existing normal-step
+initialization/completion lifecycle. This follows the measured four-frame
+candidate in `99e607b`, retained as a 1.5x fallback. New action IDs AA–AD and
+internal speed index 5 are appended; existing IDs and speed rows retain their
+values. Per-tile collision, stairs, ledges, rails, map triggers and other
+special movement continue through existing paths. Rules outside the city map
+group retain their existing behavior. `GetPlayerSpeed()` keeps its field-input
+category `PLAYER_SPEED_FASTER`, so native Start/A/B handling remains enabled.
+The three-frame path has no emulator-speed setting.
+
+`python3 tools/taichung_gameplay/test-bike-shortcut.py` extracts the actual
+changed native functions into explicit engine fixtures and checks fresh/held
+presses, stale modal input, simultaneous actions, midstep expiry/cancellation,
+context/terrain gates, task capacity, collision fallback and selected speeds.
+The strict host compile/check passes. These fixtures do not measure a real
+ROM's travel time or prove registered-item cleanup; runtime evidence and its
+exact ROM are recorded in the handoff separately.
